From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:29:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] milkflint tools: record what the MT7986 and its firmware tell
 the driver

No fake can say what the real chip and its firmware answer, so let the
driver record it. Built with MT76_RECORD=1 in the environment (which
defines CONFIG_MT76_RECORD) and loaded with mt76.record=1 or 2, mt76
records from module load into one buffer in RAM (mt76.record_kb, 16 MiB):
- every register access through the MMIO bus ops: read, write,
  read-modify-write (value read and written), copies; the address is the
  offset in the register window, as the reg_rr/reg_wr tracepoints show
  it (the router image has no ftrace);
- every firmware command: id, payload, the raw reply skb and the
  parser's result, firmware download chunks by their length only;
- rx events other than data frames (tx-free, TXS, rx vectors, firmware
  events), the MT798x SoC SKU word, the EEPROM at registration and each
  channel change.

debugfs mt76_record/data has the recording (format in record.h);
mt76_record/control has the state and takes "up", "stop" and
"mark <text>". Mode 1 stops when bring-up is over (record_settle seconds
after the last channel change, or "up"), mode 2 records record_secs
more seconds of traffic. A full buffer stops it.

Without MT76_RECORD every hook is an empty inline, record.o is empty and
the driver is unchanged; mt76_mmio_rmw() and __mt76_mcu_send_firmware()
are restructured to have one place for a hook, with the same accesses,
tracepoints and results. milkflint's KUnit build always has the
recorder.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
